f 1046 #edb/ref 17592186158141 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157490 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186051016 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157490 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "b01ba0f8-312d-5711-866c-f427e34e9a49" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158141 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158144 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158141 #edb/ref 1061 27487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158141 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0aaacbc4-1148-50d7-a5cf-50570ae0a925" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158141 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158142 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158142 #edb/ref 1061 27488 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158142 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a6d6c9e7-5ab7-5550-b9f5-44b7f15426b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158142 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157298 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158143 #edb/ref 1061 27489 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158143 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "659f011d-8169-5c22-b20a-0f19271882cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158143 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157299 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158144 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158147 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158144 #edb/ref 1061 27493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158144 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cd3a2583-f9bf-5dcc-9d80-5572b021e950" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158144 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157300 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158147 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158142 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158147 #edb/ref 1061 27531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158147 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "098b5c2c-68e3-5938-bf5f-621a688c8ba7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158147 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158486 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we want to compute the double integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D}{{\\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}\\,\\mathrm d A}},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $D$ is a washer enclosed between two circles centered at $(0,0)$ with radii $1$ and $2,$ respectively, as shown below.\n\n![](images/47/4752982aaf806b9e3b9c4d28d7a92bd01c3ff6a68afcca4235061a01b2fbc86f.png)\n\nThe easiest way to solve this problem is to convert it to polar coordinates. Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf T$ as\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = r\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nThe preimage of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ is the rectangular region $\\Delta,$ given by \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 1 \\leq r \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and its preimage $\\Delta$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/e0/e0cc6c7f92704365c3ce15b91d85f703330b767a01f1f773b014cf61620563a3.png)\n\nThe change of variables formula for polar coordinates states \n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,dA & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}\\theta +{sin}^{2}\\theta )}\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}}\\cdot r\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating this integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,dr\\,d\\theta  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }d\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,dr \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,dr \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{r}^{3}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\pi \\cdot [{2}^{3}-{1}^{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\pi \\cdot 7 \\\\  & =\\frac{14\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D}{{\\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}\\,\\mathrm d A}} = \\dfrac{14\\pi}{3}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158486 #edb/ref 1065 4466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158486 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "26614628-ce8b-5afe-ac90-b277c7c0f887" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158486 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158487 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's get some more practice at expressing regions in polar coordinates. \n\nConsider the region enclosed by the curve $x^2+(y-2)^2=4$ and the line $y=2,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/df/dffcd94cc96c33d173704a63cb03fb0f0098bc67d6c66a855c16f59694bde8e1.png)\n\nThis region represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(0,2).$\n\nFirst, we recall the usual transformations from polar to Cartesian coordinates:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now use the above formulas to write the functions that bound our region in polar coordinates:\n\n- For $x^2+(y-2)^2 = 4,$  we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ ({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}\\theta -4r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +4 & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}\\theta +{sin}^{2}\\theta )-4r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-4\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r = 4\\sin\\theta.$\n\n- For $y=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2 \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =2 \\\\ r & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\ r & =2\\operatorname{csc}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta.$ \n\nFirst of all, note that if we draw an arrow through the region from the origin, it enters the region via the curve $r=2\\csc\\theta$ and exits via $r=4\\sin\\theta \\mathbin{:}$ \n\n![](images/b0/b09ab4e4e08dfb056964947dc866b965940b761d70c945faf001446c1df7869f.png)\n\nSecondly, our curves intersect at the points $(-2,2)$ and $(2,2).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/6a/6a132d5f8c58ce60c794595ee80c57c5b9b02e630004808cd53a7677efd022e7.png)\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r, \\theta),$ the region can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 2\\csc\\theta \\leq r \\leq 4\\sin\\theta, \\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4} \\Big\\}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158487 #edb/ref 1065 4472 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158487 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "870d0884-b353-5292-b2e5-593725024502" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158487 #edb/ref 1067 "Writing More Complex Regions in Polar Coordinates" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275627 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275627 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275627 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "10db7571-c250-563b-b925-4a7669bb0915" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275710 #edb/ref 1080 "2\\pi " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275710 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275710 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "191c25d1-e662-5470-8eea-07f7ede108e5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275831 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275831 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275831 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "26e4c4a7-016c-5aa7-b9f0-9b2d2b8e2ead" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186277003 #edb/ref 1080 "4{\\pi }^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186277003 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186277003 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a9a0fd28-3760-55bd-b4cd-551d81da6733" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186277526 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186277526 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186277526 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ea9caf25-20ec-56ed-b184-ae2940d69e0f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278164 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186275627 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278164 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186275710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278164 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186275831 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278164 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186277003 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278164 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186277526 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278164 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278164 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278164 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186275627 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278164 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f8e19673-91fc-5bda-8e2c-95da05a9da2e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278384 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278164 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278384 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161114" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278384 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278384 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/5b/5b5b5e82eb8dca9ae71c08b306ac4618500c7c3af4aa0858297d5585e055007f.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,$ where the region $D$ is enclosed between two circles centered at $O$ of radii $4$ and $6,$ as shown above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278384 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4e1a8f74-9fd7-53da-9313-229356fec0da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278384 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the annulus between the disks of radii $4$ and $6$ centered at the origin. \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16,$ we obtain $r=4.$\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=36,$ we obtain $r=6.$\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,4\\le r\\le 6,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{4}^{6}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{4}^{6}\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {[r]}_{4}^{6}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (6-4)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{[\\frac{{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta }{3}]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
